sidered best for one  black does not give up control over f6  right away  king knight is still there and two  the white knight on d5 is soon to be  given a boot  with c6 in fact the bishop on g5 is also  ready to be given a boot  the two white miners that are in black's  house  will be kicked away alright  after knight g to e7  how to take advantage of this the  tempting move for me  in this position was to put the knight  right into f6  and i imagine with all this talk so far  i've  i've shared about that f6 square many of  you went in this direction  ah the knight gets to jump in with check  black's gonna have to go uncastled  you don't want to give up the bishop for  night either and say  uh you know if if you actually go for  this white is better the rook moves  how are you ever getting rid of this guy  yeah that's not a good option what is  best here is to play king to fa in this  position black is actually better  black is the one who's generating a  threat now black's uncastled but  is ready to go with h6 bishop backing up  g5  and then win the knight let's also not  forget  black is still up upon in this position  white has yet to recapture it  what is the way to win in this position  as white  another pop quiz white to move how do  you punish black's last move  okay the winning continuation the move  played in the game is  knight takes on d4 only now  okay what's happening here let's see  for starters if knight takes knight  this is one of those painful moves  you get the piece back and you're  getting the queen  soon enough that hurts  uh let's also note what the threat here  is  let's just say black makes a dummy move  a passing move  a6 the idea here is to get rid of  the defender of e7 and then take the  knight  i guess that would be best but still the  issue here is that white is winning a  minor piece  so what do we do  bishop takes knight that's the move that  was played  the follow-up here one more pop quiz  feel free to pause the video  here we go queen takes bishop  that's cool i really like this  continuation  you're giving up the queen why well if  knight takes queen  they would follow a checkmate in two and  how cool is that  those two gaps on f6  and h6 that's checkmate  knight on f6 bishop on h6  do the job so  what does karjakin play these dark  squares are officially  broken down there is no save here  black castles walks right into  knight f6 check if the king goes to h7  we have our pick which double check do  we want  that one looks cool followed up with  made on g7  and well okay if king g7 that's what we  could do in the game king h8 is played  and now we have the final move of the  game knight to g4  karjakin resigns there are only three  moves  in this position excuse me four moves so  let's let's point them out you could  move the king to g8  you could block the check like this  block the check like that or capture the  queen  let's go through each one by one king g8  knight h6 this should this is a move  that's going to surface in  pretty much every variation from here on  out that's mate  another variation f6 we would take on  f6 king here that's me  rook takes bishop we take with the queen  favorite square for the knight again h6  and are we there yet night block okay  that's nothing gonna be we know what's  happening there  in the final variation  knight takes queen remember earlier  there was a knight on f6  and then bishop on h6  they switch roles in this variation  this time bishop f6 knight h6  once more mate this is a  beautiful way to capitalize on these two  gaps  f6 and h6 it's really really cool for me  to see this  early queen sacrifice leading to  a complete collapse of the dark squares  on the king's side  anyhow i hope you found this one  instructive as well  feel free as usual to leave any comments  in the comments section below  and i'll catch you in the next video  that's all for now take care  bye  you

